djustment.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Only C2 has a negative score-control-minus-evaluated-PPI++ mean width difference; all six other paired differences are positive and their pointwise intervals exclude zero.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"analysis_results.json#/cases\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Compared with labeled normal inference, score control is narrower in C1-C3 and wider in C4-C7. Compared with exact binomial it is narrower with negative pointwise intervals in C1-C4,C6,C7, and unresolved in C5.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"analysis_results.json#/cases\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"All seven score-control pointwise coverage intervals contain 0.95; none of these finite-sample estimates establishes nominal coverage or a finite-sample guarantee.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"analysis_results.json#/cases\", \"source_evidence/calibration/METHOD.md\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Descriptive pooled coverage is 0.9425223214285714; no iid binomial interval is attached to the condition-pooled rate.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.coverage\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/coverage\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Descriptive pooled mean_width is 0.12988779848794016; no iid binomial interval is attached to the condition-pooled rate.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.mean_width\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/mean_width\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Final evaluation status is completed and outcome is qualified, with correct=true in the qualification record. Acceptance does not establish nominal validity or implementation identity.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.correct\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation\"], \"visible_translation\": \"Completed and qualified; internal correctness field is not surfaced in visible manuscript.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Candidate diagnostics are unverified; gate-specific failure causes and condition sampling mechanisms are not supplied. No named task transfer or executed matched affine/spline ablation is evidenced.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence\", \"source_evidence/calibration/METHOD.md\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"The archived target is the binary superpopulation E[Y], with unchanged labeled outcomes and labeled/unlabeled frozen scalar scores, alpha=0.05, and no truth or condition identity supplied to inference.\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L1-L44\"], \"source_role\": \"Registry source is an archived-method path anchor only; its scalar score does not substantiate implementation details or final source identity. Inspect the readable files in evidence_refs.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Archived calibration uses two complementary labeled folds and two unlabeled folds; training-only intercept, centering/scaling, ridge/GCV and quantile knots; heldout controls are clipped to [0,1].\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L51-L102\", \"source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L123-L171\"], \"source_role\": \"Registry source is an archived-method path anchor only; its scalar score does not substantiate implementation details or final source identity. Inspect the readable files in evidence_refs.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Archived hyperparameters: training sizes <4 or score range <1e-12 retain the constant; feature scale floor 1e-8; up to three quartile knots at training size >=16; up to seven quantile knots at size>=64; penalties 0.001,0.01,0.1,1,10; GCV uses trace degrees of freedom and denominator floor 1/m.\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"HoerlKennard1970\", \"Golub1979GCV\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L58-L93\"], \"source_role\": \"Registry source is an archived-method path anchor only; its scalar score does not substantiate implementation details or final source identity. Inspect the readable files in evidence_refs.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Archived fold estimator is residual mean plus rho times unlabeled calibrated mean with rho=N/(n+N), using fixed labeled-fold-size weights. Pooled residual/control variance and Wilson-style stabilization are approximate, not a finite-sample proof; n<16 uses labeled-only exact binomial inversion by a size-only rule.\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L123-L185\", \"source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L246-L259\"], \"source_role\": \"Registry source is an archived-method path anchor only; its scalar score does not substantiate implementation details or final source identity. Inspect the readable files in evidence_refs.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"For fixed square-integrable controls under independent common-population iid samples, the stated variance decomposition, conditional-mean oracle variance, excess rho*Var(g-m)/n and unrestricted affine projection gap hold. They are not measured oracle attainment or validity of the fitted-control interval.\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/calibration/METHOD.md\", \"source_evidence/calibration/study.py#L12-L26\"], \"source_role\": \"Registry source is an archived-method path anchor only; its scalar score does not substantiate implementation details or final source identity. Inspect the readable files in evidence_refs.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"The separate unmeasured bounded construction uses empirical-Bernstein radii with log(4/delta), support lengths, singleton/full-range fallback, delta=alpha/(2K), and fixed nonnegative aggregation. A conditional union-bound coverage argument assumes independent iid common-population samples. These endpoints are not the primary returned track.\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"MaurerPontil2009\", \"Hoeffding1963\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L109-L185\", \"source_evidence/calibration/METHOD.md\"], \"source_role\": \"Registry source is an archived-method path anchor only; its scalar score does not substantiate implementation details or final source identity. Inspect the readable files in evidence_refs.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"An affine-only matched function and oracle reporting helper are present but have no supplied measured results. No new experiments, bootstrap, jackknife or sensitivity sweep were run during drafting.\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L188-L192\", \"source_evidence/calibration/study.py\", \"scripts/build_artifacts.py\"], \"source_role\": \"Registry source is an archived-method path anchor only; its scalar score does not substantiate implementation details or final source identity. Inspect the readable files in evidence_refs.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Approximate is Better than “Exact” for Interval Estimation of Binomial Proportions\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1080/00031305.1998.10480550\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"AgrestiCoull1998\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/AgrestiCoull1998.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Prediction-powered inference\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1126/science.adi6000\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Angelopoulos2023PPI\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Angelopoulos2023PPI_consulted.txt\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: PPI++: Efficient Prediction-Powered Inference\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.arxiv.2311.01453\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Angelopoulos2023PPIpp\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Angelopoulos2023PPIpp_consulted.txt\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Exploration–exploitation tradeoff using variance estimates in multi-armed bandits\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1016/j.tcs.2009.01.016\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Audibert2009\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Audibert2009_consulted.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Controlling the False Discovery Rate: A Practical and Powerful Approach to Multiple Testing\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1111/j.2517-6161.1995.tb02031.x\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"BenjaminiHochberg1995\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/BenjaminiHochberg1995.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Binomial Confidence Intervals\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1080/01621459.1983.10477938\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"BlythStill1983\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/BlythStill1983.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Interval Estimation for a Binomial Proportion\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1214/ss/1009213286\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"BrownCaiDasGupta2001\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/BrownCaiDasGupta2001.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Double/debiased machine learning for treatment and structural parameters\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1111/ectj.12097\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Chernozhukov2018DML\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Chernozhukov2018DML.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Calibration Estimators in Survey Sampling\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1080/01621459.1992.10475217\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"DevilleSarndal1992\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/DevilleSarndal1992.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1214/aos/1176344552\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Efron1979\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Efron1979.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: The Jackknife Estimate of Variance\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1214/aos/1176345462\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"EfronStein1981\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/EfronStein1981.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: An Introduction to the Bootstrap\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1007/978-1-4899-4541-9\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"EfronTibshirani1993\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/EfronTibshirani1993_consulted_excerpt.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Monte Carlo Methods in Financial Engineering\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1007/978-0-387-21617-1\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Glasserman2004\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Glasserman2004_consulted.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Generalized Cross-Validation as a Method for Choosing a Good Ridge Parameter\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1080/00401706.1979.10489751\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Golub1979GCV\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Golub1979GCV.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: The Elements of Statistical Learning\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1007/978-0-387-84858-7\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Hastie2009\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Hastie2009_consulted.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Probability Inequalities for Sums of Bounded Random Variables\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1080/01621459.1963.10500830\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Hoeffding1963\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Hoeffding1963.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1080/00401706.1970.10488634\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"HoerlKennard1970\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/HoerlKennard1970.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Statistical Analysis with Missing Data\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1002/9781119013563\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"LittleRubin2019\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/LittleRubin2019.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Empirical Bernstein Bounds and Sample Variance Penalization\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.arxiv.0907.3740\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"MaurerPontil2009\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/MaurerPontil2009_consulted.txt\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Two-sided confidence intervals for the single proportion: comparison of seven methods\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1002/(sici)1097-0258(19980430)17:8<857::aid-sim777>3.0.co;2-e\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Newcombe1998\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Newcombe1998.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Root-N-Consistent Semiparametric Regression\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.2307/1912705\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Robinson1988\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Robinson1988.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Model Assisted Survey Sampling\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1007/978-1-4612-4378-6\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Sarndal1992\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Sarndal1992_consulted_excerpt.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Cross-Validatory Choice and Assessment of Statistical Predictions\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1111/j.2517-6161.1974.tb00994.x\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Stone1974\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Stone1974.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: The Probable Error of a Mean\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1093/biomet/6.1.1\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Student1908\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Student1908.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Asymptotic Statistics\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1017/cbo9780511802256\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"VanDerVaart1998\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/VanDerVaart1998.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\nSTRATEGY central_question \"With labels, unlabeled scores, and the predictor held fixed within each comparison, what interval-width gains and coverage limitations does honest score calibration exhibit for a binary population mean relative to prediction-powered and labeled-only intervals?\"\nSTRATEGY central_thesis \"Across seven evaluated binary-mean conditions, honest score calibration has a small paired width advantage over the evaluated PPI++ comparator in only one condition, while rare-outcome interval collapse exposes why width comparisons must be accompanied by coverage uncertainty and an exact-binomial reference.\"\nSTRATEGY notes_for_writer [\"Scope: this is a standalone empirical/methodological limitations study, not a search diary, architecture contribution, or new universally valid CI procedure. Neutral cases are separate evidence blocks under one common binary-mean question; do not name a dataset from the task's optional menu or infer case mechanisms from observed gains.\", \"Qualification audit, private provenance: final.qualification.status=completed, final.qualification.outcome=qualified, final.qualification.correct=true. No failed final qualification is reported in this run. Do not overwrite these with search flags or imply that qualification proves scientific validity. Gate-specific diagnostics beyond the supplied fields are unknown. If later authoritative evidence reports failed qualification, preserve that outcome and limitations in the paper, review, audit, and revisions without guessing whether the cause is performance, validity, or adaptivity.\", \"Private provenance only: use result.top_program_01 to locate the selected source. With no downstream_study or study.* sources, omit search-score deltas, scored/unscored attempt counts, and original-incumbent superiority claims. Internal pipeline labels and source paths belong in private grounding or anonymized reproducibility records, not reader-facing titles or claims.\", \"Target discipline: E[Y] is the binary superpopulation mean. Inputs are fixed across methods within a repetition; uncertainty is assessed over repeated draws, not by silently changing the target to the realized finite-pool mean. Common-population iid sampling and predictor independence are assumptions, not properties established for unidentified generators. The finite-N oracle formulas and fold-variance approximation must be distinguished.\", \"Theory can explain the opportunity, not attribute the measured effect: for a fixed control, state the variance decomposition and conditional-mean optimum under explicit assumptions; the estimated clipped spline family need not attain the oracle. Include no nonlinear-versus-affine efficacy claim without measured matched ablation. Treat the safety construction as separate and unmeasured throughout captions, abstract, review, and conclusions.\", \"Uncertainty: retain the supplied per-condition coverage intervals and paired width-difference intervals, label them pointwise, and state that seven conditions and multiple baselines were examined. Existing non-rejection flags are not equivalence tests or guarantees. Describe seeds as seeded repetitions; independence of all within-seed conditions is not established. Endpoint replay flags and unverified candidate diagnostics do not certify statistical coverage.\", \"Venue/layout: read VENUE.md and the inspected templates/iclr/paper.tex plus paper.sty; the practical geometry is one 5.5-inch text column by 9 inches. All three required figures use the full text width, not a nonexistent two-column figure* geometry. Target readable final labels of at least 8.5 points for plots and 9 points for the overview. Keep diagrams shallow and use a 2-by-2 grid rather than seven-category crowded panels.\", \"Body budget: target 8.5 pages excluding references: abstract/introduction and concrete contributions 1.25; formulation/related work plus early overview 1.25; method and limited theoretical analysis 2.0; evaluation design and provenance limits 1.0; direct coverage/width comparison including compact table 1.75; rare-outcome analysis 0.75; limitations/conclusion 0.5. These allocations sum to 8.5 and include figures. Use available space for estimand clarity, fold isolation, comparator coverage, and failure analysis rather than more unsupported experiments. Final body must be 8-9 pages; if exactly 8 pages, the last must be at least 75% occupied. This is a plan, not a measured pagination result.\", \"Suggested thesis-first sections: Introduction; Problem and Related Work; Honest Frozen-Score Controls; Evaluation Design; Coverage and Width Across Conditions; Rare-Outcome Limits; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Explain the missing nonlinear ablation, absent safety measurement, unidentified generators/sample sizes, narrow binary target, and no distribution-shift or new-domain validation explicitly. No padding or chronological branch descriptions.\", \"Main evidence table: include all five evaluated inferential methods; omit the [0,1] sanity interval from headline efficiency comparisons. Use compact condition labels and split coverage/width blocks if needed. Keep the one C2 improvement, six PPI++ widenings, C5 collapse counts, and candidate coverage caveat in the main body. Do not infer evaluator internals from field names such as valid_gain_supported.\", \"Supplement: after the references use clearpage, appendix, and a substantive Supplementary Information section with the planned subsections. Route later authoritative appendix-ranked analyses there without changing this strategy. Keep evidence essential to the central thesis in the main text. Include the separate LLM Usage disclosure required by the venue.\", \"Template repair for later drafting: the staged ICLR scaffold contains a named author and iclrfinalcopy, so use anonymous review mode and remove author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, and identifying links. Preserve any orchestrator-owned watermark block. The staged style provides practical geometry but does not itself establish official ICLR 2026 template provenance; verify the required venue version through permitted official material before final submission.\", \"Literature limitation: references.bib/references_provenance.json presently supply DML and GCV metadata but omit the central permitted PPI/PPI++ starter works and exact-binomial/empirical-Bernstein foundations. Before a final manuscript, obtain and actually consult exact allowlisted starter/foundational sources; verify bibliographic metadata, including GCV's venue field. Do not invent missing citation keys or cite unrelated retrieval hits as padding. Never search citation networks, guess the prohibited paper, or inspect ambiguous follow-up material. This strategy stage performs no literature retrieval and makes no claim that metadata equals source consultation.\", \"Vector-first delivery: quantitative plotting scripts must emit PDF and PNG at 300 DPI, and LaTeX must reference PDF whenever available. Preserve editable concept-figure output and follow the orchestrator's generate-concept-figure handoff later, not during strategy generation. Raster-only replacements require regeneration at least 2400 pixels wide full-width or 1200 pixels wide column-width; never upscale. Keep method colors and validity labels consistent across figures.\", \"Evidence gaps are limitations, not promised experiments. Available endpoints suffice for the planned comparisons and CDFs; no new data collection, predictor retraining, safety-track rerun, or later ablation is necessary to make the narrow thesis readable. Do not report these missing studies as completed. Reproduction of the sealed data-generating experiment remains incomplete even if every table can be regenerated from the released records.\", \"Planning stage only: write neither paper.tex nor claims_grounding.json, generate no figures, and do not run final-paper validation. Validate only this strategy's JSON structure, source references, consistency, and output boundaries.\"]\nDATA paper_profile \"standalone\"\nDATA presentation_contract {\"forbidden_visible_terms\": [\"eligible\", \"correct and eligible\", \"correctness rate\", \"worker_01\", \"worker_02\", \"combined_score\", \"rubric\"], \"internal_fields\": [\"correct\", \"eligible\"], \"preferred_terms\": {\"idea\": \"model variant\", \"ideas\": \"model variants\", \"implementation\": \"configuration\", \"implementations\": \"configurations\", \"program\": \"seeded run\", \"programs\": \"seeded runs\"}, \"run_unit\": \"seeded_run\", \"threshold_rate_rule\": \"If a thresholded rate appears in the visible paper, keep one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse a single label for different task-specific thresholds.\", \"variant_unit\": \"model_variant\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '365,425p' paper.tex; python - <<'PY'\nimport json,re\nfrom pathlib import Path\nmanuscript=Path('paper.tex').read_text()\nkeys=[]\nfor match in re.finditer(r'\\\\\\\\cite\\\\w*\\\\*?(?:\\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\]]*\\\\])*\\\\{([\"'^}]+)'\"\\\\}',manuscript):\n keys.extend(key.strip() for key in match.group(1).split(','))\nbib_keys=set(re.findall(r'@\\\\w+\\\\s*\\\\{\\\\s*([\"'^,]+)'\"',Path('references.bib').read_text()))\nprovenance=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nprov_keys={item.get('citation_key') for item in provenance['references']}\nprint('CITED KEYS:', ', '.join(sorted(set(keys))))\nprint('Missing from bibliography:',sorted(set(keys)-bib_keys))\nprint('Missing from provenance:',sorted(set(keys)-prov_keys))\nclaims=json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())['claims']\nprint('Claims type:',type(claims).__name__,'count:',len(claims))\nif isinstance(claims,list):\n print('Claim schema:',list(claims[0]))\n for claim in claims:\n  if any(word in str(claim).lower() for word in ['literature','clopper','oracle']):\n   print(json.dumps(claim,ensure_ascii=False)[:650])\nelse:\n print('Claim keys:',list(claims)[:10])\nprint('Provenance scopes:')\nfor item in provenance['references']:\n print(item['citation_key'],':',item.get('consultation_scope','unspecified'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '365,425p' paper.tex; python - <<'PY'\nimport json,re\nfrom pathlib import Path\nmanuscript=Path('paper.tex').read_text()\nkeys=[]\nfor match in re.finditer(r'\\\\\\\\cite\\\\w*\\\\*?(?:\\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\]]*\\\\])*\\\\{([\"'^}]+)'\"\\\\}',manuscript):\n keys.extend(key.strip() for key in match.group(1).split(','))\nbib_keys=set(re.findall(r'@\\\\w+\\\\s*\\\\{\\\\s*([\"'^,]+)'\"',Path('references.bib').read_text()))\nprovenance=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nprov_keys={item.get('citation_key') for item in provenance['references']}\nprint('CITED KEYS:', ', '.join(sorted(set(keys))))\nprint('Missing from bibliography:',sorted(set(keys)-bib_keys))\nprint('Missing from provenance:',sorted(set(keys)-prov_keys))\nclaims=json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())['claims']\nprint('Claims type:',type(claims).__name__,'count:',len(claims))\nif isinstance(claims,list):\n print('Claim schema:',list(claims[0]))\n for claim in claims:\n  if any(word in str(claim).lower() for word in ['literature','clopper','oracle']):\n   print(json.dumps(claim,ensure_ascii=False)[:650])\nelse:\n print('Claim keys:',list(claims)[:10])\nprint('Provenance scopes:')\nfor item in provenance['references']:\n print(item['citation_key'],':',item.get('consultation_scope','unspecified'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"C6 & Exact binomial & -0.005638 & [-0.005995, -0.005281] \\\\\n\\midrule\nC7 & PPI++ & +0.002681 & [+0.002112, +0.003251] \\\\\nC7 & PPI & -0.025593 & [-0.026689, -0.024498] \\\\\nC7 & Label normal & +0.002409 & [+0.001840, +0.002978] \\\\\nC7 & Exact binomial & -0.003766 & [-0.004334, -0.003197] \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{longtable}\n\nThe full-range interval is $[0,1]$ in the records and covers the recorded binary-mean truths. It is a check on the endpoint/target convention, not a competitive width baseline. Raw width ratios can be dominated by zero or near-zero denominators in C5. We therefore do not interpret the retained pooled ratio as a multiplicative efficiency advantage, and do not infer how any denominator regularization was implemented.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/width_ratio_ppiplus]]\n\n\\subsection{Calibration and Interval Implementation}\n\\label{sec:implementation}\nReadable source copies are supplied under \\path{source_evidence/calibration/}. The entrypoint specification declares \\path{program.py}; the functions below describe that archive. The final evaluation's recorded source-tree hash is not independently reconstructed from these copies. Thus, these locations substantiate source-level choices, not a stronger assertion that every sealed endpoint came from an identical file tree.\n\n\\paragraph{Training-only model choice.}\nThe basis contains the raw score and positive-part hinges $(S-\\kappa)_+$. The training outcome mean supplies the intercept. Each feature is centered and scaled on the training fold; standard deviations are floored at $10^{-8}$. For a training count $m$, a penalty $\\lambda$ solves\n\\begin{equation}\n\\widehat\\beta_\\lambda=(Z^\\top Z/m+\\lambda I)^{-1}Z^\\top(Y-\\overline Y)/m.\n\\end{equation}\nThe effective degrees of freedom are $d=1+\\operatorname{tr}\\{(Z^\\top Z/m+\\lambda I)^{-1}Z^\\top Z/m\\}$. The GCV denominator is $\\max(1-d/m,1/m)^2$. This score selects among the available basis/penalty pairs and the constant fit \\citep{HoerlKennard1970,Golub1979GCV}. The smallest training size for nonconstant fitting is four; score range below $10^{-12}$ keeps the intercept. None of these thresholds is a measured tuning optimum.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L58-L93]]\n\n\\paragraph{Randomization and output.}\nThe reset seed initializes the local random generator used for permutations. State is cleared between repetitions; labels are not pooled across them. Only the predefined outcome and score arrays enter inference. The interface reports an estimate and bounded endpoints, not calibration diagnostics. In the exact small-$n$ branch, beta quantiles implement equal-tailed binomial inversion. Otherwise the reported point estimate is the clipped corrected mean, while the interval center is $c$ from Equation~\\ref{eq:stabilized}. These need not be identical. The stabilization is not an exact Wilson interval, even though its algebra resembles score-based boundary adjustments \\citep{Newcombe1998,AgrestiCoull1998}.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L195-L259]]\n\n\\paragraph{Unmeasured functions.}\nThe archive exposes \\texttt{fit\\_affine\\_calibrator} and an internal affine option with the same clipping and penalty grid but no hinges. The reporting helper also defines an oracle variance calculation. Neither function supplies an executed controlled comparison in the sealed evidence. The preparation note's statement that experiments had not yet been run describes preparation time; it does not negate the subsequently supplied endpoint records. Conversely, later records do not supply missing affine or bounded-track results.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L188-L192]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/study.py#L12-L26]]\n\n\\subsection{Fixed-Control Variance and Separate Safety Argument}\n\\label{sec:theory}\n\\paragraph{Variance decomposition.}\nAssume the iid sampling model from the main text, $n,N$ fixed, and a fixed square-integrable $g$. Write $Y=m(S)+\\varepsilon$, where $\\E[\\varepsilon\\mid S]=0$. Independence between the labeled and unlabeled samples gives Equation~\\ref{eq:fixed}. Let $h=g-m$. Orthogonality of $\\varepsilon$ and score functions yields\n\\begin{align}\n\\Var(Y-\\rho g)&=\\E[\\Var(Y\\mid S)]+\\Var\\{(1-\\rho)m-\\rho h\\},\\\\\n\\Var(g)&=\\Var(m+h).\n\\end{align}\nThe cross term cancels because $(1-\\rho)/n=\\rho/N$. The remaining coefficients are\n\\begin{equation}\n\\frac{(1-\\rho)^2}{n}+\\frac{\\rho^2}{N}=\\frac{1}{n+N},\\qquad\n\\rho^2\\left(\\frac1n+\\frac1N\\right)=\\frac{\\rho}{n}.\n\\end{equation}\nThis proves Equations~\\ref{eq:oracle}--\\ref{eq:excess}. Additive constants in $g$ cancel from the estimator, consistent with the variance rather than uncentered squared-error excess. If $\\Pi_{\\mathrm{aff}}m$ is the $L^2$ projection onto unrestricted affine functions of $S$, including an intercept, the oracle reduction relative to the best such affine control is\n\\begin{equation}\n\\frac{N}{n(n+N)}\\E\\bigl[(m-\\Pi_{\\mathrm{aff}}m)^2\\bigr].\n\\end{equation}\nThe projection residual has mean zero. Constant scores reduce the affine space to constants. This calculation concerns unrestricted population controls; the clipped, regularized fitted affine family is not that same class. No recorded condition is shown to attain this reduction.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/METHOD.md#Oracle-frontier-and-asymptotic-qualifications]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/study.py#L12-L26]]\n\n\\paragraph{Why fitted-control inference requires more.}\nFor fixed $g$, elementary variance algebra suffices. For estimated $g_{-k}$, conditional sample separation preserves the correction's expectation under the iid model, but unconditional covariances across folds remain. First-order inference requires additional control of fitted-function error and variance estimation, as emphasized more generally in semiparametric theory \\citep{Robinson1988,VanDerVaart1998,Chernozhukov2018DML}. The archive does not establish the required rates for its finite dictionary/GCV rule in each condition. The Efron--Stein perspective also emphasizes accounting for estimator sensitivity rather than treating fitted components as fixed \\citep{EfronStein1981}. We use no such inequality to certify the returned interval.\n\n\\paragraph{Bounded safety construction.}\nFor a held-out component sample $A_1,\\ldots,A_m$ with known range length $B$, the archived radius is\nCITED KEYS: AgrestiCoull1998, Angelopoulos2023PPI, Angelopoulos2023PPIpp, Audibert2009, BenjaminiHochberg1995, BlythStill1983, BrownCaiDasGupta2001, Chernozhukov2018DML, DevilleSarndal1992, Efron1979, EfronStein1981, EfronTibshirani1993, Glasserman2004, Golub1979GCV, Hastie2009, Hoeffding1963, HoerlKennard1970, LittleRubin2019, MaurerPontil2009, Newcombe1998, Robinson1988, Sarndal1992, Stone1974, Student1908, VanDerVaart1998\nMissing from bibliography: []\nMissing from provenance: []\nClaims type: list count: 110\nClaim schema: ['claim', 'source_ids', 'citation_keys', 'evidence_refs']\n{\"claim\": \"For fixed square-integrable controls under independent common-population iid samples, the stated variance decomposition, conditional-mean oracle variance, excess rho*Var(g-m)/n and unrestricted affine projection gap hold. They are not measured oracle attainment or validity of the fitted-control interval.\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/calibration/METHOD.md\", \"source_evidence/calibration/study.py#L12-L26\"], \"source_role\": \"Registry source is an archived-method path anchor only; its scalar score does not substantiate implementation details or final source identity. I\n{\"claim\": \"An affine-only matched function and oracle reporting helper are present but have no supplied measured results. No new experiments, bootstrap, jackknife or sensitivity sweep were run during drafting.\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L188-L192\", \"source_evidence/calibration/study.py\", \"scripts/build_artifacts.py\"], \"source_role\": \"Registry source is an archived-method path anchor only; its scalar score does not substantiate implementation details or final source identity. Inspect the readable files in evidence_refs.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Approximate is Better than “Exact” for Interval Estimation of Binomial Proportions\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1080/00031305.1998.10480550\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"AgrestiCoull1998\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/AgrestiCoull1998.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Prediction-powered inference\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1126/science.adi6000\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Angelopoulos2023PPI\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Angelopoulos2023PPI_consulted.txt\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: PPI++: Efficient Prediction-Powered Inference\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.arxiv.2311.01453\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Angelopoulos2023PPIpp\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Angelopoulos2023PPIpp_consulted.txt\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Exploration–exploitation tradeoff using variance estimates in multi-armed bandits\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1016/j.tcs.2009.01.016\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Audibert2009\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/Audibert2009_consulted.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/; no numerical experimental claims sourced to references.\"}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature context: Controlling the False Discovery Rate: A Practical and Powerful Approach to Multiple Testing\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.doi.10.1111/j.2517-6161.1995.tb02031.x\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"BenjaminiHochberg1995\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"literature/BenjaminiHochberg1995.json\"], \"scope\": \"Bibliographic identity and consultation scope recorded in literature/