{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '407,433p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,85p' paper_strategy.json; tail -n 18 memory.md; ls global_memory/conclusions* 2>/dev/null; python - <<'PY'\nimport json,re,hashlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\ntext=Path('paper.tex').read_text()\ncited={key.strip() for match in re.finditer(r'\\\\\\\\cite\\\\w*\\\\*?(?:\\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\]]*\\\\])*\\\\{([\"'^}]+)'\"\\\\}',text) for key in match.group(1).split(',')}\nbib=set(re.findall(r'@\\\\w+\\\\s*\\\\{\\\\s*([\"'^,]+)'\"',Path('references.bib').read_text()))\nprovenance=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nprovenance_keys={entry.get('citation_key') for entry in provenance['references']}\nclaims=json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())['claims']\nprint('COVERAGE:',json.dumps({'distinct_cited_keys':len(cited),'bib_entries':len(bib),'provenance_entries':len(provenance['references']),'missing_bib_keys':sorted(cited-bib),'missing_provenance_keys':sorted(cited-provenance_keys),'grounded_claims':len(claims)}))\nfor name in ['paper.tex','references.bib','references_provenance.json']:\n print('SHA256',name,hashlib.sha256(Path(name).read_bytes()).hexdigest())\ndata=json.loads(Path('paper_data.json').read_text())\nfinal=data.get('final_evaluation',{})\nprint('FINAL EVALUATION',json.dumps({key:final.get(key) for key in ['status','outcome','metrics']})[:1200])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '407,433p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,85p' paper_strategy.json; tail -n 18 memory.md; ls global_memory/conclusions* 2>/dev/null; python - <<'PY'\nimport json,re,hashlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\ntext=Path('paper.tex').read_text()\ncited={key.strip() for match in re.finditer(r'\\\\\\\\cite\\\\w*\\\\*?(?:\\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\]]*\\\\])*\\\\{([\"'^}]+)'\"\\\\}',text) for key in match.group(1).split(',')}\nbib=set(re.findall(r'@\\\\w+\\\\s*\\\\{\\\\s*([\"'^,]+)'\"',Path('references.bib').read_text()))\nprovenance=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nprovenance_keys={entry.get('citation_key') for entry in provenance['references']}\nclaims=json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())['claims']\nprint('COVERAGE:',json.dumps({'distinct_cited_keys':len(cited),'bib_entries':len(bib),'provenance_entries':len(provenance['references']),'missing_bib_keys':sorted(cited-bib),'missing_provenance_keys':sorted(cited-provenance_keys),'grounded_claims':len(claims)}))\nfor name in ['paper.tex','references.bib','references_provenance.json']:\n print('SHA256',name,hashlib.sha256(Path(name).read_bytes()).hexdigest())\ndata=json.loads(Path('paper_data.json').read_text())\nfinal=data.get('final_evaluation',{})\nprint('FINAL EVALUATION',json.dumps({key:final.get(key) for key in ['status','outcome','metrics']})[:1200])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\\end{align}\nThe cross term cancels because $(1-\\rho)/n=\\rho/N$. The remaining coefficients are\n\\begin{equation}\n\\frac{(1-\\rho)^2}{n}+\\frac{\\rho^2}{N}=\\frac{1}{n+N},\\qquad\n\\rho^2\\left(\\frac1n+\\frac1N\\right)=\\frac{\\rho}{n}.\n\\end{equation}\nThis proves Equations~\\ref{eq:oracle}--\\ref{eq:excess}. Additive constants in $g$ cancel from the estimator, consistent with the variance rather than uncentered squared-error excess. If $\\Pi_{\\mathrm{aff}}m$ is the $L^2$ projection onto unrestricted affine functions of $S$, including an intercept, the oracle reduction relative to the best such affine control is\n\\begin{equation}\n\\frac{N}{n(n+N)}\\E\\bigl[(m-\\Pi_{\\mathrm{aff}}m)^2\\bigr].\n\\end{equation}\nThe projection residual has mean zero. Constant scores reduce the affine space to constants. This calculation concerns unrestricted population controls; the clipped, regularized fitted affine family is not that same class. No recorded condition is shown to attain this reduction.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/METHOD.md#Oracle-frontier-and-asymptotic-qualifications]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/study.py#L12-L26]]\n\n\\paragraph{Why fitted-control inference requires more.}\nFor fixed $g$, elementary variance algebra suffices. For estimated $g_{-k}$, conditional sample separation preserves the correction's expectation under the iid model, but unconditional covariances across folds remain. First-order inference requires additional control of fitted-function error and variance estimation, as emphasized more generally in semiparametric theory \\citep{Robinson1988,VanDerVaart1998,Chernozhukov2018DML}. The archive does not establish the required rates for its finite dictionary/GCV rule in each condition. The Efron--Stein perspective also emphasizes accounting for estimator sensitivity rather than treating fitted components as fixed \\citep{EfronStein1981}. We use no such inequality to certify the returned interval.\n\n\\paragraph{Bounded safety construction.}\nFor a held-out component sample $A_1,\\ldots,A_m$ with known range length $B$, the archived radius is\n\\begin{equation}\nb(A,\\delta)=\\min\\left\\{B,\\sqrt{\\frac{2s_A^2\\log(4/\\delta)}{m}}\n+\\frac{7B\\log(4/\\delta)}{3(m-1)}\\right\\},\\qquad m\\geq2.\n\\label{eq:eb}\n\\end{equation}\nFor a singleton, it uses $B$. This is a conservative two-sided empirical-Bernstein form \\citep{MaurerPontil2009}; known boundedness, as in classical concentration arguments \\citep{Hoeffding1963}, is essential. Capping at $B$ cannot invalidate a bound because both the sample and population means lie in the support interval. The variance-sensitive term can be useful, but the additive range term can make the interval substantially wider than an asymptotic one. Related variance-aware bounds are studied in other settings \\citep{Audibert2009}; those sequential results are not empirical evidence here.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L109-L119]]\n\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Frozen-Score Mean Inference: Width Gains and Coverage Limits\",\n  \"central_question\": \"With labels, unlabeled scores, and the predictor held fixed within each comparison, what interval-width gains and coverage limitations does honest score calibration exhibit for a binary population mean relative to prediction-powered and labeled-only intervals?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"Across seven evaluated binary-mean conditions, honest score calibration has a small paired width advantage over the evaluated PPI++ comparator in only one condition, while rare-outcome interval collapse exposes why width comparisons must be accompanied by coverage uncertainty and an exact-binomial reference.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The strongest evidence is the completed final evaluation: 256 seeded repetitions, seven conditions, paired interval endpoints, and per-condition coverage and width-difference uncertainty. These support a standalone study of conditional gains, costs, and failure modes, not universal superiority or an identified causal benefit of nonlinear calibration. The primary asymptotic interval and the separately implemented, unmeasured finite-sample safety interval must remain distinct. Cases share one research question and estimator interface; they are not separately named application studies. Final qualification is reported independently of search selection and is not a theorem of nominal coverage.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"recentered\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Fresh framing, not an edit: no resolved edit-context files, previous_paper, aggregated, or extra_tasks directories are present. The enum recentered denotes centering the task's broad opportunity question on the actual condition-specific final evidence, not changing an existing manuscript. A generic improvement story is rejected; a broad cross-method search narrative lacks measured comparisons; the selected standalone story is a coverage-aware width study of the evaluated score-control method.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No prior manuscript or newly staged edit-mode implementation is available, so preserving, broadening, or replacing a prior title or figure plan is not applicable.\",\n    \"The selected candidate source under results/ideas/02_honest_nonlinear_score_controls_with_a_finite_sample_safety_track/program_1/src identifies the binary superpopulation target, two-fold calibrator, stabilized asymptotic primary interval, and separate safety construction. result.top_program_01 links that candidate path to search selection; the final evidence records a frozen program-tree digest, but no separate final source tree is staged for file-by-file comparison.\",\n    \"paper_data.json.final_evaluation independently reports completed and qualified, with 256 episodes. Its case summaries, endpoints, and uncertainty replace the source preparation notes' earlier absence of executed evidence; those notes still correctly distinguish the primary and unmeasured safety tracks.\",\n    \"paper_data.json.downstream_study and study.* grounding sources are absent. Do not manufacture an original-incumbent comparison, search improvement, or scored/unscored attempt accounting from memory or aggregate campaign totals.\",\n    \"The bibliography is present but central PPI/PPI++, exact-binomial, and empirical-Bernstein sources are missing. Existing DML and GCV metadata are relevant leads, not proof of having read those works. Off-topic retrieval records do not enlarge the study's scientific scope.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Honest frozen-score controls\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The studied estimator fits clipped ridge affine/spline score transforms on complementary labeled folds, uses rho=N/(n+N), and returns a Wilson-style stabilized asymptotic Wald interval, with a sample-size-only exact-binomial branch below 16 labels. This is the evaluated primary method, not a universally finite-sample-valid procedure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evaluated PPI++ mean interval\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Primary efficiency comparator with paired endpoints and uncertainty in all seven conditions; identifies the single small width reduction and six width increases. Describe the evaluated comparator, not all possible PPI++ implementations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evaluated PPI mean interval\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Original prediction-powered reference has measured endpoints and reveals different width/coverage behavior from PPI++, especially in the rare-outcome condition. Include in the compact main results table and rare-outcome figure, with complete comparisons in the supplement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Labeled-only asymptotic mean interval\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Separates benefits over label-only normal inference from benefits over prediction-powered inference; its rare-outcome collapse is measured rather than an untested failure hypothesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Labeled-only exact binomial interval\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Provides a finite-sample coverage reference under independent Bernoulli sampling. Its conservatism and width are essential context, not a matched-validity justification for the asymptotic primary method.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Bounded componentwise safety construction\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Source implements separate empirical-Bernstein residual/control bounds and fixed-weight aggregation. Explain assumptions and union-bound logic as a theoretical alternative, never as an evaluated series or a reason to label the returned interval exact.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Use final.evidence.episodes as the numerical source, especially episodes[0].solution.case_summaries and all episodes[*].solution.cases. Summary coverage, paired mean differences, and zero-width counts were checked against the 256 records for each case. Retain neutral labels C1-C7 because generators, named datasets, and case-specific sample sizes are not identified in these records.\",\n    \"Primary paired comparison: candidate-minus-PPI++ mean width difference is -0.00030173260155952255 in C2, with reported 95% interval [-0.0004003584986521721, -0.000203106704466873]. The other six differences are positive. These are condition-specific width findings, not a multiplicity-adjusted universal conclusion or a causal spline ablation.\",\n    \"Display per-condition empirical coverage with reported uncertainty alongside width differences. Candidate coverage ranges from 0.9296875 to 0.95703125, and all seven reported candidate coverage intervals include 0.95. Non-rejection is not evidence that nominal coverage has been established.\",\n    \"C5 is explicitly the low-mean condition: recorded truth 0.006800000000000005. Evaluated PPI++ and labeled-only asymptotic coverage are 0.55078125, each with 115 zero-width intervals. Candidate coverage is 0.9296875 with no zero-width intervals; exact-binomial coverage is 0.99609375. The candidate-minus-exact width difference is -0.000046988429386779555 with interval [-0.00032065914173324357, 0.0002266822829596845], so this comparison is inconclusive on mean width.\",\n    \"Explain the observed label-only width reductions in C1-C3 and the small widening relative to labeled-only asymptotic intervals in C4, C6, and C7. Put PPI, PPI++, both labeled-only references, and candidate on a single compact comparison table, split into readable blocks if necessary.\",\n    \"The final aggregate coverage 0.9425223214285714 and mean width 0.12988779848794016 are descriptive summaries, not evidence of exact 95% validity. Do not calculate a binomial uncertainty interval by treating all seven within-seed conditions as independent repetitions.\",\n    \"Do not lead with the aggregate width ratio 64175.10539621905. Near-zero and zero comparator widths make ratio summaries misleading; emphasize raw paired width differences, interval distributions, coverage, and collapse counts instead. Do not infer the evaluator's ratio regularization formula.\",\n    \"No measured affine-versus-spline ablation, safety-track width, oracle attainment, named real-data result, or matched comparison to the original search incumbent exists in the supplied paper evidence. Do not fill these gaps with unscored methods or source-only functions.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"An early full-text-width conceptual overview shows immutable labels/scores, complementary-fold score calibration, residual correction, the evaluated asymptotic interval, and a visually separate unmeasured safety construction.\",\n    \"A full-text-width four-panel comparison pairs empirical coverage with candidate-minus-baseline width differences for C1-C4 and C5-C7; show the nominal coverage line and zero-difference line, with pointwise uncertainty and fixed method colors.\",\n    \"A full-text-width two-panel rare-outcome diagnostic presents empirical interval-width distributions and zero-width counts in C5, making collapsed intervals visible instead of rewarding them through ratios.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"frozen_score_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Make the estimand, immutable-input restriction, fold isolation, and distinction between evaluated primary and unmeasured safety intervals immediately legible.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"information_flow\",\n      \"metric\": \"none_conceptual\",\n      \"split_by\": \"evaluated_primary_vs_unmeasured_safety\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"The inspected ICLR article style is single-column with a 5.5-inch text width and 9-inch text height. Use all 5.5 inches for a shallow five-object diagram; a half-text-width placement would crowd the fold-isolation labels. Place on page 1 or 2, preferably page 2 near the problem definition.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 5,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"content_spec\": \"Five grouped objects: immutable labeled/unlabeled score inputs; complementary-fold fit/evaluate pairing; finite-N residual correction for E[Y]; evaluated stabilized asymptotic interval with size-only exact fallback; separate unmeasured bounded safety interval. Use solid primary and dashed safety connectors. No extra labels, predictor retraining, chronological arrows, or empirical-gain badge.\",\n      \"target_size_inches\": [5.5, 1.8],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9,\n- evidence: final.qualification.* reports completed/qualified/true; final.evidence.episodes contains 256 paired repetitions and seven conditions. downstream_study and task_names absent. Read candidate source: primary stabilized asymptotic interval, separate unmeasured safety track. Existing bibliography contains largely irrelevant retrieval hits and will be replaced with directly consulted permitted foundations.\n- next_action: Prepare inspectable source copies, derive paired plots and tables, consult exact permitted literature, draft anonymous ICLR manuscript with explicit reproduction limits.\n\n### 2026-11-07T05:13:45.084050+00:00 | step=draft | scope=paper\n- summary: Drafted the standalone condition-specific width/coverage study and generated paired evidence tables and quantitative plots.\n- evidence: analysis_results.json reproduces all seven final summary mean width differences and confidence limits. The narrative preserves C2-only PPI++ narrowing, all six widenings, C5 collapses/undercoverage, and the unresolved C5 exact-binomial comparison. Code claims cite inspectable source_evidence copies and explicitly do not certify final source identity. Official ICLR 2026 template retrieved directly from ICLR/Master-Template/iclr2026. Concept rendering is reserved for the orchestrator with a guarded placeholder.\n- next_action: Finish exact-source bibliography consultation, write claim mappings and reproducibility guide, compile and audit evidence links and rendered layout.\n\n### 2026-11-07T05:32:36.573557+00:00 | step=verify | scope=paper\n- summary: Manuscript and claim map pass grounding and section checks; local rendered audit has eight body pages with substantive Supplementary Information and 25 consulted references.\n- evidence: Validated 110 claims against final.* sources, the archived-method path anchor plus inspectable source files, and 25 reference-provenance IDs. All seven case summaries reproduced from endpoints. Scientific-evidence reports zero dangling links. Figure manifest validates against the canonical schema and strategy density limits. Local body-page text extent is about 98%, not the parent authoritative geometry metric. Canonical figure entrypoint lacks src.orchestrator; canonical compile checker rejects a system TeX symlink across approved roots and system pdftotext is absent. Sanitized LaTeX/BibTeX compilation and independent rendered-SI checks succeed. Original Wilson/Clopper-Pearson and Newey-McFadden records were excluded when substantive text access failed; consulted interval-comparison and asymptotic-statistics sources replace them.\n- next_action: Run final evidence verification and visual inspection; record completed artifacts and preserve concept-orchestrator ownership and reproduction/source-identity caveats.\n\n### 2026-11-07T05:35:48.523469+00:00 | step=final | scope=paper\n- summary: paper.tex and claims_grounding.json are consistent with grounded inputs; the anonymous ICLR 2026 draft, PDF, quantitative figures, source archive, and reproduction guide are written.\n- evidence: Final PDF has eight body pages, two reference pages and substantive Supplementary Information including LLM Usage. All 25 citation keys validate, 110 claims map to valid evidence/provenance IDs, 32 manuscript JSON pointers and 17 source-line locators resolve in the local audit, and scientific-evidence has zero dangling references. Final qualification is reported independently as completed/qualified without turning it into a nominal-validity guarantee. C2-only PPI++ narrowing, six widenings, C5 undercoverage/collapse, inconclusive C5 exact-binomial width comparison, missing generators/sample sizes/ablation, and unverified final source identity remain explicit. Visual inspection confirms the final body page is substantially filled and figures use vector exports. No new scientific evaluation was run.\n- next_action: Orchestrator should render the reserved concept figure synchronously and perform its authoritative final compile/layout validation. Canonical tooling limitations are documented in local_validation_report.json; end-to-end experiment reproduction remains unavailable rather than fabricated.\n\nglobal_memory/conclusions.md\nglobal_memory/conclusions_history.jsonl\nglobal_memory/conclusions_other_workers.md\nCOVERAGE: {\"distinct_cited_keys\": 25, \"bib_entries\": 25, \"provenance_entries\": 25, \"missing_bib_keys\": [], \"missing_provenance_keys\": [], \"grounded_claims\": 110}\nSHA256 paper.tex defe4c73532d20bfcce1c5619327a171c924059f89ec11c452e35815ec79a82e\nSHA256 references.bib d3c8f134c58e51c55be66427c4b2ed3df16445baafa5eaaa3486d826894791ec\nSHA256 references_provenance.json 4c7ce1248061ddc3aa006c652e633f2968b807a8e79ef66e68bd999d1c2a3bc6\nFINAL EVALUATION {\"status\": \"completed\", \"outcome\": \"qualified\", \"metrics\": {\"combined_score\": 0.8701122015120599, \"control_pass_rate\": 1.0, \"correct\": 1, \"coverage\": 0.9425223214285714, \"mean_width\": 0.12988779848794016, \"width_ratio_ppiplus\": 64175.10539621905}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
